{
  "beruf": "buero",
  "title": "Office and admin",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Office jobs in France use a lot of written French, formal phrases on the phone and careful handling of customer data. This deck covers the office and its equipment, daily tasks, meetings and schedules, data protection and filing, and what you say to callers, visitors and colleagues. Use vous with customers and callers; colleagues often say tu, but follow what the others do.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in an office in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Assistant administratif / assistante administrative",
    "Agent d'accueil / agente d'accueil",
    "Secrétaire",
    "Standardiste",
    "Employé de bureau / employée de bureau"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "On the phone, give the company and your name: « Entreprise Lumière, bonjour, Nadia à l'appareil. » Then ask: « Je vous écoute. » or « C'est de la part de qui ? »",
    "With customers and callers use vous; with colleagues tu is common, but listen to what the others say first. In emails, « Madame, Monsieur, » at the start and « Cordialement. » at the end are the safe formal choice.",
    "Do not leave personal data on your desk or send it by e-mail without need. If you are not sure, ask your manager: « Je peux envoyer ça par e-mail ? »",
    "Rules on data, working hours and contracts differ between employers. Ask your employer, France Travail or a union if you are not sure what applies to you. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
